  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how polar ocean observing has expanded across three technological eras. In the earliest “ship era,” an ice-capable research vessel lowers instruments through patchy sea ice, representing relatively sparse, point-based measurements; the panel notes about 107,000 stations sampled. The “satellite era” shifts to broad, continuous remote sensing, with a satellite scanning a large swath over the ice-covered ocean and producing huge spatial coverage, indicated by roughly 1.5 × 10^12 surface samples. \n\nThe “autonomous era” adds multiple robotic and animal-borne platforms that operate in and under sea ice. A red uncrewed surface vehicle surveys the surface, a seal-like marine mammal appears to carry a sensor package, vertical profilers collect CTD profiles in the water column, and an underwater glider samples beneath the ice edge. The labels emphasize the scale of these observations: about 250,000 km of USV surface transects, 300,000 mammal profiles, 773,000 CTD profiles, and 40,000 glider profiles.\n\nOverall, the figure shows a progression from limited ship-based sampling to massive satellite coverage and then to autonomous systems that fill critical gaps, especially in difficult, ice-covered regions and through the full water column.",
